[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was marked known: one issue, one fix queued. Read report by report, it
holds three problems. Each problem got its own ticket.

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {issues} />

## Where each one stands

Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October. The tickets are closed to match.

| problem | outcome | ticket |
|---|---|---|
| `parser` | fixed: `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales` | closed, completed |
| `hint` | won't fix: the booking-v3 form is retired | closed, not planned |
| `move` | filed under `parser` by the desk, closed with it | closed, completed |

<Callout kind="warn">
The parser fix reads dates the tenant's way. It does not explain a date that moves by one day.
If one-day moves keep coming, reopen the <code>move</code> ticket.
</Callout>

## The three problems

**`parser`: the shared parser reads day-first dates month first.** A day-first tenant books
7 April and the invite says 4 July. Most of #4471 is this.

**`hint`: booking-v3 prints an `MM/DD/YYYY` hint to day-first tenants.** Their people type month
first, as the hint says, and the form reads the date day first. The dates come out swapped, so it
looks like `parser`, but the parser fix does not touch it. It shows only through the form channel
at tenants that run booking-v3.

**`move`: the invite moves by one day.** No month is flipped: the 12 May invite shows 11 May, a
client in Lisbon gets Tuesday while one in Toronto gets Monday. That points at time zones, not at
date order. The desk already read these as a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`), but the cause
table on #4471 counts them under `parser`.

## How the reports were placed

- One id is one report. The export comes in overlapping pages, so some rows appear twice; the
  counts drop the repeats.
- The cause comes from the tenant's locale and booking form (`#lib/kb/causes`), not from the
  intake tag. The tag is a guess from the reporter's browser.
- Counts are the desk's own, on the export as it is. The one-day moves stay counted under the
  cause they are filed under, and also get the `move` row and ticket (`#lib/kb/problems`).

## Why they were merged

All three came in under the same subject lines, and [decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)
merges on subject alone. Here that hid two problems inside a ticket marked known. A new decision
record could add a check on the cause; this page does not change 0001.

